        "# Lecture 6.1: Random Variables Motivation\n",
        "\n",
        "## Random Variables: Motivation\n",
        "\n",
        "We begin with informal \"experiments\" to motivate our formal measure-theoretic framework.\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {#exm-coin-toss}\n",
        "**Coin Tossing.**\n",
        "Consider tossing a fair coin $N$ times. The individual outcomes are sequences of heads ($H$) and tails ($T$), such as $HTHH$. We might be interested in a summary numerical measurement, such as $X = \\text{\\# heads}$. In this specific outcome, $X = 3$. We can then consider events defined by conditions on $X$, such as $\\{X \\le 3\\}$ or $\\{X \\ge 2\\}$.\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {#exm-dart-throw}\n",
        "**Dart Board.**\n",
        "Consider throwing a dart at a circular dartboard of radius $R$. The point where the dart lands can be represented in polar coordinates as $(r, \\theta)$. A natural summary quantity is $R$, the distance from the center. An outcome might be $(r, \\theta) = \\left(0.15, \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)$ (or $(0.15, 0)$), yielding $R = 0.15$. We are then interested in events such as $\\{R \\le 0.25\\}$.\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "In general, each experiment has an outcome $\\omega$, belonging to the set of all possible outcomes called the **sample space**, denoted by $\\Omega$. \n",
        "\n",
        "Probability is a measure of the likelihood of an **event** $E \\subseteq \\Omega$, which is a set of outcomes.\n"
      ],
