      "source": [
        "The underlying sample space $(\\Omega, \\mathcal{F}, \\mathbb{P})$ is often \"inaccessible\" directly:\n",
        "\n",
        "* Outcomes are elements $\\omega \\in \\Omega$.\n",
        "* Events are measurable subsets of $\\Omega$, i.e., elements of $\\mathcal{F}$.\n",
        "\n",
        "A function $X: \\Omega \\to S$ maps the inaccessible sample space to a manageable \"state space\" $S$. \n",
        "\n",
        "* The state space $S$ could be $\\mathbb{C}$.\n",
        "* $S$ is usually $\\mathbb{R}$.\n",
        "* If $S = \\mathbb{R}^d$, we usually call $X$ a **random vector**.\n",
        "\n",
        ":::{#rem-random-variable}\n",
        "## Random Variable versus Random Function\n",
        "The term \"random variable\" is a misnomer. A random variable is not a variable in the sense of algebra, but rather a function. It is a function that takes an outcome $\\omega \\in \\Omega$ and returns a real number $X(\\omega) \\in \\mathbb{R}$. The \"random\" part refers to the fact that the outcome $\\omega$ is random, and thus $X(\\omega)$ is also random. Historically, we should perhaps call them \"random functions,\" but the very old \"variable\" terminology has stuck since its usage by Laplace in the early 19th century.\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "To perform probability calculations, we need to be able to compute probabilities of sets formed by conditions on $X$, such as:\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\{X \\le 1\\} := \\{\\omega \\in \\Omega : X(\\omega) \\le 1\\}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "## Formal Definition of a Random Variable\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {#def-random-variable}\n",
        "### Random Variable\n",
        "Let $(\\Omega, \\mathcal{F}, \\mathbb{P})$ be a probability space. A function $X: \\Omega \\to \\mathbb{R}$ is a **random variable** if it is $\\mathcal{F}$-measurable; that is, for every $t \\in \\mathbb{R}$,\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\{X \\le t\\} := \\{\\omega \\in \\Omega : X(\\omega) \\le t\\} \\in \\mathcal{F}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "- Note that the definition of a random variable depends on the underlying probability space $(\\Omega, \\mathcal{F}, \\mathbb{P})$. A random variable is not just a function from $\\Omega$ to $\\mathbb{R}$; it must also be measurable with respect to the sigma-algebra $\\mathcal{F}$.\n",
        "\n",
        "- The measurability condition ensures that we can assign probabilities to events defined by the random variable, such as $\\{X \\le t\\}$, using the probability measure $\\mathbb{P}$. \n",
        "\n",
        "- We can also denote $\\{X \\le t\\}$ as\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\{X \\le t\\} = X^{-1}((-\\infty, t]) = \\{\\omega \\in \\Omega : X(\\omega) \\in (-\\infty, t]\\}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "## Cumulative Distribution Functions (CDFs)\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {#def-cdf}\n",
        "### Cumulative Distribution Function\n",
        "Let $X: \\Omega \\to \\mathbb{R}$ be a random variable on $(\\Omega, \\mathcal{F}, \\mathbb{P})$. The **cumulative distribution function (CDF)** of $X$ is the function $F_X: \\mathbb{R} \\to \\mathbb{R}$ defined by:\n",
        "$$\n",
        "F_X(t) := \\mathbb{P}(X \\le t) = \\mathbb{P}\\bigl(\\{\\omega \\in \\Omega : X(\\omega) \\le t\\}\\bigr).\n",
        "$$\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {#prp-cdf-properties}\n",
        "## Properties of the CDF\n",
        "The function $F_X: \\mathbb{R} \\to [0, 1]$ satisfies the following properties:\n",
        "\n",
        "1. $F_X$ is non-decreasing: if $s \\le t$, then $F_X(s) \\le F_X(t)$.\n",
        "2. $F_X$ is right-continuous: for all $t \\in \\mathbb{R}$, $\\lim_{n \\to \\infty} F_X(t_n) = F_X(t)$ for any sequence $t_n \\downarrow t$.\n",
        "3. Limits at infinity:\n",
        "   $$\n",
        "   \\lim_{t \\to -\\infty} F_X(t) = 0 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad \\lim_{t \\to +\\infty} F_X(t) = 1.\n",
        "   $$\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.proof}\n",
        "## Proof of @prp-cdf-properties\n",
        "We establish each property using continuity of the probability measure $\\mathbb{P}$:\n",
        "\n",
        "1. **Non-decreasing:** Let $s \\le t$. Then $\\{\\omega : X(\\omega) \\le s\\} \\subseteq \\{\\omega : X(\\omega) \\le t\\}$. By monotonicity of measure $\\mathbb{P}$:\n",
        "   $$\n",
        "   F_X(s) = \\mathbb{P}(X \\le s) \\le \\mathbb{P}(X \\le t) = F_X(t).\n",
        "   $$\n",
        "\n",
        "2. **Right-continuity:** Let $t_n \\downarrow t$ (i.e., $t_1 \\ge t_2 \\ge \\dots \\to t$). Define $A_n = \\{X \\le t_n\\}$. The sequence of events $\\{A_n\\}_{n=1}^\\infty$ is non-increasing ($A_n \\supseteq A_{n+1}$) and satisfies:\n",
        "   $$\n",
        "   \\bigcap_{n=1}^\\infty A_n = \\{X \\le t\\}.\n",
        "   $$\n",
        "   By continuity of measure from above (since $\\mathbb{P}(A_1) \\le 1 < \\infty$):\n",
        "   $$\n",
        "   F_X(t_n) = \\mathbb{P}(X \\le t_n) \\downarrow \\mathbb{P}(X \\le t) = F_X(t).\n",
        "   $$\n",
        "\n",
        "3. **Limits at infinity:**\n",
        "   * Consider $b_n \\downarrow -\\infty$. The events $B_n = \\{X \\le b_n\\}$ satisfy $B_n \\supseteq B_{n+1}$ and $\\bigcap_{n=1}^\\infty B_n = \\emptyset$. Thus:\n",
        "     $$\n",
        "     \\lim_{n \\to \\infty} F_X(b_n) = \\mathbb{P}(\\emptyset) = 0.\n",
        "     $$\n",
        "   * Consider $c_n \\uparrow +\\infty$. The events $C_n = \\{X \\le c_n\\}$ satisfy $C_n \\subseteq C_{n+1}$ and $\\bigcup_{n=1}^\\infty C_n = \\Omega$. By continuity of measure from below:\n",
        "     $$\n",
        "     \\lim_{n \\to \\infty} F_X(c_n) = \\mathbb{P}(\\Omega) = 1.\n",
        "     $$\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "By the **Carathéodory Extension Theorem**, $F_X$ uniquely determines a Borel probability measure $\\mu_X$ on $(\\mathbb{R}, \\mathcal{B}(\\mathbb{R}))$, defined on intervals by $\\mu_X((a, b]) = F_X(b) - F_X(a)$.\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {#def-law}\n",
        "### Probability Distribution / Law\n",
        "The measure $\\mu_X$ is called the **probability distribution** (or **law**) of $X$.\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {#rem-knowledge-of-law}\n",
        "- Note that the probability distribution $\\mu_X$ is a measure on the Borel sigma-algebra $\\mathcal{B}(\\mathbb{R})$, which is generated by intervals of the form $(a, b]$. On the other hand, $\\mathbb{P}$ is a measure on the sigma-algebra $\\mathcal{F}$ of the sample space $\\Omega$. \n",
        "- Consider $\\mu_X((a, b])$:\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\begin{align*}\n",
        "\\mu_X((a, b]) &= F_X(b) - F_X(a)\\\\\n",
        "&= \\mathbb{P}(X \\le b) - \\mathbb{P}(X \\le a)\\\\\n",
        "&= \\mathbb{P}(\\{\\omega : X(\\omega) \\le b\\}) - \\mathbb{P}(\\{\\omega : X(\\omega) \\le a\\})\\\\\n",
        "&= \\mathbb{P}(\\{\\omega : a < X(\\omega) \\le b\\})\\\\\n",
        "&= \\mathbb{P}(X^{-1}((a, b]))\\\\\n",
        "&= \\mathbb{P}\\circ X^{-1}((a, b]).\n",
        "\\end{align*}\n",
        "$$\n",
        "- Often $\\mu_X$ is all we will really know about $X$. And even more often, we won't even know $\\mu_X$, but will only have some limited clues about it.\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "## (Great) Expectations\n",
        "\n",
        "## Finite and Countable Sample Spaces\n",
        "\n",
        "Can we get a \"snapshot\" number that tells us something about this distribution?\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {#def-finite-expectation}\n",
        "### Expectation on Finite Sample Spaces\n",
        "Let $\\Omega = \\{\\omega_1, \\omega_2, \\dots, \\omega_N\\}$ be a finite sample space, and set $\\mathcal{F} = 2^\\Omega$. The probability of any event $E \\subseteq \\Omega$ is given by:\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\mathbb{P}(E) = \\sum_{\\omega \\in E} \\mathbb{P}(\\{\\omega\\}) = \\sum_{\\omega \\in \\Omega} \\mathbf{1}_{E}(\\omega) \\mathbb{P}(\\{\\omega\\}),\n",
        "$$\n",
        "where $\\mathbf{1}_A(\\omega)$ is the indicator function:\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\mathbf{1}_A(\\omega) = \\begin{cases} 1 & \\text{if } \\omega \\in A, \\\\ 0 & \\text{if } \\omega \\notin A. \\end{cases}\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "If $X: \\Omega \\to \\mathbb{R}$ is a random variable, its CDF is:\n",
        "$$\n",
        "F_X(t) = \\mathbb{P}(X \\le t) = \\sum_{\\omega \\in \\Omega} \\mathbf{1}_{\\{X(\\omega) \\le t\\}} \\mathbb{P}(\\{\\omega\\}).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "We define the **expected value** (or weighted average) of $X$ as:\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\mathbb{E}(X) := \\sum_{\\omega \\in \\Omega} X(\\omega) \\mathbb{P}(\\{\\omega\\}). \\tag{$\\star$}\n",
        "$$\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {#exm-three-coins}\n",
        "**Three Coin Tosses.**\n",
        "Toss a fair coin 3 times, and let $X = \\text{\\# Heads}$.\n",
        "The sample space is:\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\Omega = \\{HHH, HHT, HTH, THH, HTT, THT, TTH, TTT\\},\n",
        "$$\n",
        "with $\\mathbb{P}(\\{\\omega\\}) = \\frac{1}{8}$ for all $\\omega \\in \\Omega$.\n",
        "\n",
        "The values of $X(\\omega)$ are:\n",
        "\n",
        "* $X(HHH) = 3$ (1 outcome)\n",
        "* $X(HHT) = X(HTH) = X(THH) = 2$ (3 outcomes)\n",
        "* $X(HTT) = X(THT) = X(TTH) = 1$ (3 outcomes)\n",
        "* $X(TTT) = 0$ (1 outcome)\n",
        "\n",
        "Applying formula $(\\star)$:\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\begin{align*}\n",
        "\\mathbb{E}(X) &= \\frac{1}{8} \\left( 3 \\cdot 1 + 2 \\cdot 3 + 1 \\cdot 3 + 0 \\cdot 1 \\right) \\\\\n",
        "&= \\frac{1}{8}(3 + 6 + 3 + 0) = \\frac{12}{8} = \\frac{3}{2} = 1.5.\n",
        "\\end{align*}\n",
        "$$\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "## Limitations and Generalizations\n",
        "\n",
        "The definition $(\\star)$ makes perfect sense if $\\Omega$ is finite. It is also okay if $\\Omega$ is countably infinite.\n",
        "\n",
        "However, formula $(\\star)$ **will not help us** if $\\mathbb{P}(\\{\\omega\\}) = 0$ for all $\\omega \\in \\Omega$, which is typical for continuous sample spaces (e.g., throwing a dart).\n",
        "\n",
        "### The Undergraduate Probability Approach\n",
        "\n",
        "In introductory probability, one grouping transformation is performed:\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\begin{align*}\n",
        "\\sum_{\\omega \\in \\Omega} X(\\omega) \\mathbb{P}(\\{\\omega\\}) &= \\sum_{t} \\sum_{\\omega : X(\\omega) = t} X(\\omega) \\mathbb{P}(\\{\\omega\\}) \\\\\n",
        "&= \\sum_{t} t \\sum_{\\omega : X(\\omega) = t} \\mathbb{P}(\\{\\omega\\}) \\\\\n",
        "&= \\sum_{t} t \\, \\mathbb{P}(X = t).\n",
        "\\end{align*}\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "One then attempts to find an analogue of this sum in the \"continuous\" setting by replacing sums with integrals: $\\sum_t t \\, \\mathbb{P}(X=t) \\rightsquigarrow \\int t f_X(t) \\, dt$.\n",
        "\n",
        "**Problems with this approach:** There are many. For instance, proving fundamental structural properties like linearity $\\mathbb{E}(X + Y) = \\mathbb{E}(X) + \\mathbb{E}(Y)$ becomes cumbersome or requires separating discrete and continuous cases.\n",
        "\n",
        "### The Measure-Theoretic Solution\n",
        "\n",
        "We will develop the right generalization of $(\\star)$ to work in **any** general probability space $(\\Omega, \\mathcal{F}, \\mathbb{P})$: the **Lebesgue Integral**.\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\mathbb{E}(X) = \\int_{\\Omega} X \\, d\\mathbb{P}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
